# Numbering sources

Each numbering source is a separate process. SipOut, Runexis, UIS, Aurora Telecom, MTS Exolve, Voximplant, and MCN Telecom are the sources in this release. SipOut reads stay in the SipOut rule. Runexis reads stay in the Runexis rule. UIS reads stay in the UIS rule. Aurora Telecom reads stay in the Aurora Telecom rule. MTS Exolve reads stay in the MTS Exolve rule. Voximplant reads stay in the Voximplant rule. MCN Telecom reads stay in the MCN Telecom rule. Do not invent a source, a host, or a method.

SipOut owns `numbering_sync_state` and `numbering_sync_stage`. Runexis owns `runexis_sync_state` and `runexis_sync_stage`. UIS owns `uis_sync_state` and `uis_sync_stage`. Aurora Telecom owns `aurora_sync_state`, `aurora_sync_stage`, and `aurora_files`. MTS Exolve owns `exolve_sync_state` and `exolve_sync_stage`. Voximplant owns `voximplant_sync_state` and `voximplant_sync_stage`. MCN Telecom owns `mcn_sync_state` and `mcn_sync_stage`. Each state row stays `id = 1` with `CHECK (id = 1)`. A source does not insert into the other source's tables and does not drop the other check. Each source has its own module, its own client, its own `globalThis` slot, and its own text log.

`free_numbers` and `purchased_numbers` are the shared catalogs. A publish deletes and inserts only the rows of that source's provider literal, in one transaction. SipOut replaces its rows in both catalogs. Runexis replaces its rows in both catalogs. UIS replaces its rows in both catalogs. Aurora Telecom replaces its rows in both catalogs. MTS Exolve replaces its rows in both catalogs. Voximplant replaces its rows in both catalogs. MCN Telecom replaces its rows in both catalogs. The Aurora Telecom provider literal is `Aurora Telecom`. The MTS Exolve provider literal is `MTS Exolve`. The Voximplant provider literal is `Voximplant`. The MCN Telecom provider literal is `MCN Telecom`. A load of SipOut, Runexis, UIS, MTS Exolve, Voximplant, or MCN Telecom reads that source's purchased numbers before its free numbers. A failed purchased list does not start the free-number read. Aurora Telecom reads the saved http or https links. A successful Aurora Telecom load writes the free rows and clears the purchased Aurora Telecom rows. An empty Aurora Telecom set does not publish. Both HTTP lists must succeed before either catalog is replaced. A failed read leaves the catalogs. The provider name is a literal in the SQL. Do not accept a source name from a request, a flag, or a setting.

The SipOut walk, the SipOut access check, and the SipOut key save share one in-memory holder. The key save does not take the database lease. Do not split that holder. The Runexis walk, the Runexis access check, and the Runexis password save share a different holder. The UIS walk, the UIS access check, and the UIS key save share a third holder. The Aurora Telecom link save, the link check, and the walk share a fourth holder. The link save and the link check do not take the database lease. The MTS Exolve walk, the MTS Exolve access check, and the MTS Exolve key save share a fifth holder. The key save does not take the database lease. The Voximplant walk, the Voximplant access check, and the Voximplant key save share a sixth holder. The key save and the access check do not take the database lease. The MCN Telecom walk, the MCN Telecom access check, and the MCN Telecom token save share a seventh holder. The key save and the access check do not take the database lease. `SIGTERM` sets one stop flag. A walk that sees it does not publish. Do not add a second listener for that signal.

Tariff resplit updates ABC, the local number, city, and region on every row of both catalogs. It locks `numbering_sync_state` `id = 1` first, then `runexis_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `uis_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `aurora_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `exolve_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `voximplant_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `mcn_sync_state` `id = 1`. A fresh heartbeat on any lease waits. It does not call a numbering source and it does not download Aurora Telecom links. The resplit is batches of 1000, a one-second pause, and a batch transaction of at most 60 seconds. It does not write mask, client, operator, or geography. A tariff file increments `resplit_epoch` and `mask_epoch` in the snapshot transaction. Any error waits 5 seconds and repeats the same id. There is no give-up.

The draft fill calls the shared tariff fragment before the catalog lock. The catalog copy writes the tariff cells, the provider prices, and empty mask, premium, and client cells. It does not call `rtu_mask_lookup`, `cdr_tariff_geo`, or `geography_digit_split`. Operator and geography stay empty. The same transaction increments that provider's epoch on `numbering_catalog_state`. Aurora Telecom uses `aurora_epoch` and `aurora_done`. MTS Exolve uses `exolve_epoch` and `exolve_done`. Voximplant uses `voximplant_epoch` and `voximplant_done`. MCN Telecom uses `mcn_epoch` and `mcn_done`. Those counters start at 0. Adding them does not change `decorate_generation`. A mask file increments `mask_epoch` in the snapshot transaction. A phone snapshot increments `client_epoch` only when the set of endpoint number and «Описание» changes, and stores that hash. An unchanged set stores the hash when it is still empty and does not bump. Each bump clears only its own cursor phases. The cursor is the phase, the provider, the last committed id, and the epoch. The next start reads it from the database. The cursor write is in the same transaction as the row update and only when that epoch is unchanged. A mask batch and a provider batch add that batch to the per-provider parsed counters in the same update. Resplit, client, and bootstrap do not. A tariff or mask bump zeros those counters. The provider epoch statement zeros only that provider's counter. Any error waits 5 seconds and repeats the same id. There is no give-up. Done stamps are written only when the epoch is unchanged. The order is resplit, then a full mask pass, then a provider whose epoch is ahead, then clients. The queue stays busy until every epoch is caught up. A pass sets the matching done stamp only when the epoch is still the value it read. Decorate uses the same lock order, in batches of 1000 with a one-second pause, and a batch transaction of at most 60 seconds. It waits on a fresh heartbeat. The first start still runs one full pass when `decorate_generation` is 0. A future source uses the same fragment, the same copy, and its own epoch literal.

The seven sources also run as one chain, in page order: SipOut, Runexis, UIS, Aurora Telecom, MTS Exolve, Voximplant, MCN Telecom. The chain calls the existing start of each source. It does not add a vendor request. It waits until that source's holder is released and the decorate queue is idle before the next start. There is no extra pause between sources and no chain-wide timeout. «Уже выполняется.» and the vacuum sentence retry the same source. A missing or unreadable secret skips that source. Any other start error moves on. The result is the new `last_finished_at`. A cancel sentence or process stop does not open the tail. Cancel during publishing still masks that source and continues. A second chain does not start. While the chain runs, a card load is refused. Access check and secret save stay on the same holder. The daily run is 22:00 UTC. A process start does not catch a window that has already begun. A chain already running at 22:00 is not queued again. Restart does not resume a chain. Do not add a second `SIGTERM` listener.

Do not write to a numbering-source API unless a task names that exact write. Aurora Telecom may send only the GET named in the Aurora Telecom rule. Do not log a key, a password, a token, a query, a URL, or a CSV body.
